**Flexbox is one-dimensional: it lays items out along a single row or column and lets content size drive the layout. Grid is two-dimensional: you define rows and columns and place items into them, so the layout drives the content.**

**Reach for flexbox when**
- Aligning items in a single line: navbars, toolbars, button groups, a card's footer.
- You want items to size to their content and share leftover space (`flex: 1`).
- Wrapping is okay to be ragged (each wrapped line is laid out independently).

**Reach for grid when**
- You need things to line up in both directions: page shells, dashboards, image galleries, forms with aligned labels.
- You want explicit tracks, gaps and named areas.
- You want a responsive grid without media queries.

```css
/* Flex: toolbar with a spacer */
.toolbar { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; }
.toolbar .spacer { flex: 1; }

/* Grid: responsive cards, no media queries */
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}
```

**Subgrid** (`grid-template-rows: subgrid`) lets nested items align to the parent's tracks, e.g. card titles, bodies and buttons lining up across a row of cards of different lengths. That used to require fixed heights or JavaScript.

**In practice** you combine them: grid for the page and the card list, flex inside each card. A useful test: if the last row of wrapped items should align with the columns above, it is a grid; if it should just flow, it is flex.

**The modern answer is grid or flex on the parent: `display: grid; place-items: center;` centers a child in both axes. Which technique is right depends on whether you are centering text, a block of known width, or something positioned on top of other content.**

```css
/* 1. Grid: shortest */
.parent { display: grid; place-items: center; min-height: 100dvh; }

/* 2. Flex */
.parent { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; }

/* 3. Auto margins inside flex or grid */
.parent { display: flex; }
.child { margin: auto; }

/* 4. Overlay on top of other content */
.modal { position: fixed; inset: 0; margin: auto; width: 400px; height: fit-content; }

/* 5. Absolute + transform (unknown size, legacy) */
.child { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); }
```

**Other cases**
- **Inline content / text:** `text-align: center` on the parent.
- **A block of fixed width horizontally:** `margin-inline: auto`.
- **Block layout, no flex/grid:** `align-content: center` now works on plain block containers in modern browsers.

**Gotchas**
- Vertical centering needs the parent to have height; a parent that shrinks to its content has nothing to center within (hence `min-height`).
- `translate(-50%, -50%)` can land on half pixels and blur text.
- With flex centering, content taller than the container overflows on *both* sides and the top becomes unscrollable; `justify-content: safe center` or `margin: auto` avoids that.

**`display: none` removes the element from layout and the accessibility tree. `visibility: hidden` keeps its space but hides it and removes it from the accessibility tree and interaction. `opacity: 0` keeps space and stays fully interactive and readable by screen readers, it is just transparent.**

| | Takes space | Clickable / focusable | Screen reader | Animatable |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `display: none` | No | No | Hidden | Not with plain transitions |
| `visibility: hidden` | Yes | No | Hidden | Yes (flips at end) |
| `opacity: 0` | Yes | Yes | Announced | Yes, smoothly |

**Mechanism**
- `display: none` skips the element (and children) in the render tree, so changing it triggers layout.
- `visibility` is inherited, and a child can override it with `visibility: visible`; `display: none` cannot be overridden by children.
- `opacity` is composited on the GPU, so fading is cheap, but the invisible element can still swallow clicks.

```css
/* Fade out without leaving a clickable ghost */
.toast { transition: opacity .2s, visibility .2s; }
.toast.hidden { opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; }

/* Visually hidden but still read by screen readers */
.sr-only {
  position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap;
}
```

**Modern note:** transitions to and from `display: none` are now possible with `transition-behavior: allow-discrete` and `@starting-style`. The `hidden` attribute is `display: none`; `hidden="until-found"` keeps content findable by Ctrl+F.

**Build mobile-first with fluid layouts (grid, flex, `clamp()`, relative units), then add breakpoints only where the design breaks. Media queries respond to the viewport; container queries respond to the size of a component's container, so a component adapts wherever it is placed.**

**Mobile-first**
- Base styles target small screens; `@media (min-width: ...)` layers on enhancements.
- Small screens get the least CSS and the simplest layout, which tends to be faster and easier to maintain.
- Many layouts need no breakpoint at all: `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr))`, `flex-wrap`, `clamp()` for type.

```css
.card { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }

/* Media query: page-level layout */
@media (min-width: 64rem) {
  .page { grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr; }
}

/* Container query: the card adapts to its slot */
.card-slot { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card { grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr; }
}
```

**Why container queries matter:** the same card might sit in a wide main column and a narrow sidebar on the same viewport. A media query cannot tell those apart; a container query can. Container query units (`cqi`, `cqw`) let type scale with the component.

**Still use media queries for** page shell layout, and for user preferences: `prefers-color-scheme`, `prefers-reduced-motion`, `hover`, `pointer: coarse`.

**Gotchas**
- `container-type: inline-size` applies size containment, so the container cannot size itself from its children's width.
- An element cannot query itself; it queries its nearest container ancestor.
- Do not forget the viewport meta tag: `width=device-width, initial-scale=1`.

**A pseudo-class (single colon, like `:hover`, `:focus-visible`, `:nth-child()`) selects an existing element in a particular state or position. A pseudo-element (double colon, like `::before`, `::placeholder`) styles a part of an element or generated content that is not in the DOM. `:has()` is a relational pseudo-class: it selects an element based on its descendants or following siblings, the long-missing "parent selector".**

**Pseudo-classes:** state (`:hover`, `:checked`, `:disabled`, `:user-invalid`), structure (`:first-child`, `:nth-of-type()`), logic (`:is()`, `:where()`, `:not()`).

**Pseudo-elements:** `::before`/`::after` (need `content`), `::first-line`, `::marker`, `::selection`, `::backdrop`. They are not focusable or selectable by JavaScript queries, and screen readers may read generated `content`, so keep it decorative.

```css
/* Style a card differently if it contains an image */
.card:has(img) { grid-template-rows: 12rem auto; }

/* Highlight a field wrapper when its input is invalid after interaction */
.field:has(input:user-invalid) { border-color: var(--danger); }

/* Disable page scroll while a dialog is open */
body:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden; }

/* Previous-sibling style: the label before a focused input */
label:has(+ input:focus-visible) { color: var(--accent); }
```

**Why :has() is a big deal:** it replaces JavaScript that toggled classes on parents (form validation wrappers, "has image" variants, layout changes when a sidebar is present). It is supported in all modern browsers.

**Gotchas**
- `:has()` takes the specificity of its most specific argument.
- Very broad selectors like `*:has(...)` on large DOMs can be costly to re-evaluate; scope them.
- Prefer `:focus-visible` over `:focus` for focus rings so mouse clicks do not show them.

**They size a box from its content instead of from outside. min-content is the narrowest the box can be without overflow (the longest word or widest image), max-content is its width if nothing wraps, and fit-content is max-content clamped to the available space. They matter for hug-the-content components, content-sized grid tracks, and for understanding why flex and grid items overflow.**

**What each one does**
```text
text: "Responsive design is fun"
min-content  -> width of "Responsive" (the longest unbreakable piece)
max-content  -> width of the whole line, no wrapping
fit-content  -> max-content if it fits, otherwise the available width (then wraps)
```

**Where I use them**
```css
/* A badge or pill that hugs its text but never overflows */
.tag { inline-size: fit-content; padding-inline: 0.5em; }

/* A form where the label column is as wide as the longest label */
.form { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 0.5rem 1rem; }

/* A sidebar that hugs content up to a cap */
.layout { grid-template-columns: fit-content(18rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }

/* A figure whose caption wraps to the image width */
figure { inline-size: min-content; }
```

**The connection interviewers look for**
Flex and grid items default to `min-width: auto`, which resolves to roughly their min-content size. So a long URL, a `pre` block or a wide table sets a minimum width the item refuses to shrink below, and the layout overflows. `1fr` is `minmax(auto, 1fr)` for the same reason. The fix is `min-width: 0` on the flex item, or `minmax(0, 1fr)` in the grid template, plus overflow handling inside (`overflow-wrap: anywhere`, `overflow-x: auto`).

**Trade-offs**
- `max-content` on user content can overflow small screens; prefer `fit-content`.
- Content-based tracks require measuring content, which is more layout work on huge grids (thousands of rows); fixed tracks are cheaper for data grids.
- These keywords work with `width`, `inline-size`, `min-*` and `max-*`, and inside `minmax()` in grid.

**I define the shell once on a grid container with named areas, header, nav, main and footer, plus row and column sizes, then rearrange the whole page at a breakpoint by changing only grid-template-areas and the column template. The one thing to watch is that visual reordering does not change DOM order, so focus and reading order still follow the source.**

```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  min-block-size: 100svh;               /* full height that fits mobile toolbars */
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;    /* footer sticks to the bottom on short pages */
  grid-template-areas:
    "header"
    "main"
    "nav"
    "footer";
}
.page > header { grid-area: header; }
.page > nav    { grid-area: nav; }
.page > main   { grid-area: main; }
.page > footer { grid-area: footer; }

@media (width >= 48rem) {
  .page {
    grid-template-columns: 16rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas:
      "header header"
      "nav    main"
      "footer footer";
  }
}
```

**Why it works well**
- The template is a readable picture of the layout; responsive changes are one declaration.
- `1fr` in the row template takes the leftover height, so the footer sits at the bottom even with little content (no sticky-footer hacks).
- `minmax(0, 1fr)` for the main column stops long content (a code block, a table) from widening the page, because a plain `1fr` has an automatic minimum of the content's min-content width.
- `gap` handles spacing between regions without margin juggling.

**Accessibility caveat**
On mobile the example above places nav after main visually. If the DOM order is header, nav, main, keyboard users still tab through the nav first, which is fine here, but reordering interactive content far from its DOM position creates a confusing focus order (WCAG 1.3.2 and 2.4.3). Prefer matching visual and DOM order for interactive regions and keep a skip link to main.

**Mobile height**: `100vh` on mobile equals the large viewport (toolbars hidden), so content can sit under the URL bar; `100svh` always fits.

**Debugging**: the grid overlay in Chrome or Firefox DevTools draws the area names on the page, which makes template mistakes obvious.

**Native nesting lets you write child rules, pseudo-classes and at-rules inside a parent rule, with & referring to the parent selector, and the browser expands it at parse time with no build step. It differs from Sass in two important ways: & is a selector, not text, so BEM-style &__element concatenation does not work, and the parent is treated like :is(), which changes specificity when the parent is a selector list.**

```css
.card {
  padding: 1rem;
  & .title { font-weight: 600; }        /* .card .title */
  &:hover { border-color: var(--brand); } /* .card:hover */
  .dark & { background: var(--surface); } /* .dark .card */
  @media (width >= 48rem) { padding: 1.5rem; }
  @container (inline-size >= 30rem) { display: grid; }
}
```

**Differences from Sass**
| | Sass | Native |
|---|---|---|
| `&__title` | `.card__title` | Not supported (no string concatenation) |
| `.a, #b { .c {} }` | `.a .c, #b .c` (two selectors) | `:is(.a, #b) .c`, specificity (1,1,0) even when matching via `.a` |
| Build step | Required | None; runs in the browser |
| Variables, mixins, loops | Yes | No (use custom properties) |

Early implementations required a nested selector to start with a symbol, so `h3 {}` inside a rule needed `& h3`; current browsers accept the relaxed syntax. Writing `&` explicitly stays the clearest style and avoids surprises.

**Specificity example**
```css
.btn, #cta {
  & span { color: red; }   /* :is(.btn, #cta) span -> (1,0,1) */
}
.btn span { color: blue; } /* (0,1,1): loses, which surprises Sass users */
```

**Do I still need a preprocessor?** For nesting alone, no. Many teams keep a build-time tool (PostCSS or Lightning CSS) for minification, vendor prefixes and lowering syntax for older browsers, and drop Sass when they no longer need mixins or loops.

**Style advice**: nest one or two levels. Deep nesting raises specificity and couples CSS to DOM structure, the same problem Sass codebases had. Nesting is best for states (`&:hover`, `&[aria-expanded="true"]`) and media or container queries kept next to the rule they modify.

**Subgrid lets a nested grid adopt the row or column tracks of its parent grid instead of defining its own, so content inside separate children lines up with each other. The classic example is a row of cards: with subgrid every card's title, description and button sit on shared row tracks, so they align even when one title wraps to three lines.**

**The problem**
Each card is its own layout. A long title in one card pushes its description and button down, so buttons in a row end up at different heights. Before subgrid people used fixed heights, line clamps, JavaScript equal-height scripts, or pinned only the button with `margin-top: auto` (which aligns the bottom but not the internal rows).

**The fix**
```css
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
.card {
  grid-row: span 3;            /* the card occupies 3 parent rows */
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: subgrid; /* and uses those rows for its children */
  gap: 0.5rem;                 /* a subgrid may override the parent gap */
}
/* children: h3, p, a.button land in rows 1, 2, 3 */
```
```text
row 1 (title)  height = tallest title in that grid row
row 2 (body)   height = tallest description
row 3 (button) aligned across all cards
```
The card must **span** the parent tracks it wants to adopt; subgrid does not create tracks, it borrows them. Line names from the parent are available inside the subgrid too.

**Another real example: forms**
A form grid with `grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr` for labels and inputs. Fieldsets inside it would normally break alignment; with `grid-column: 1 / -1; display: grid; grid-template-columns: subgrid` the nested labels align with the outer label column.

**Why not display: contents?**
`display: contents` removes the card's own box, so its children participate in the parent grid directly. But you lose the card's background, border, padding and click area, and historically it caused accessibility bugs by dropping semantics from the tree. Subgrid keeps the card a real box.

**Support**: subgrid has been in all major engines since 2023, so it is safe for production. The fallback when unsupported is simply unaligned cards, which is acceptable progressive enhancement.

**Design tokens are named, platform-neutral design decisions (colours, spacing, type, radii, motion) stored as data and transformed into CSS custom properties and platform constants. For theming I use two tiers: primitive tokens for the raw palette and semantic tokens like --color-bg and --color-primary that components use; a theme, including dark mode, is just a different mapping of the semantic tokens, switched with an attribute on the html element and set before first paint.**

**The tiers**
```css
:root {
  /* primitives */
  --gray-50: oklch(0.98 0 0);
  --gray-900: oklch(0.21 0 0);
  --blue-500: oklch(0.62 0.19 259);
  --blue-600: oklch(0.55 0.2 260);

  /* semantic (light) */
  --color-bg: var(--gray-50);
  --color-fg: var(--gray-900);
  --color-primary: var(--blue-600);
  color-scheme: light;
}
[data-theme="dark"] {
  --color-bg: var(--gray-900);
  --color-fg: var(--gray-50);
  --color-primary: var(--blue-500);   /* lighter for contrast on dark */
  color-scheme: dark;
}
.button { background: var(--color-primary); color: var(--color-on-primary); }
```
Components never reference primitives, so dark mode, high contrast or a white-label brand never touches component CSS. Some systems add a third, component tier (`--button-bg`) for fine-grained overrides.

**Source of truth and distribution**
Tokens live in JSON (the W3C Design Tokens Community Group format is the emerging standard) and a tool such as Style Dictionary emits CSS variables, a Tailwind theme, TypeScript constants, and iOS and Android values. Designers can sync them from Figma variables, so design and code share names.

**Dark mode without a flash**
- Respect the system with `prefers-color-scheme`, and let users override (light, dark, system).
- Store the choice in a cookie or localStorage, and set `data-theme` before first paint: a tiny blocking inline snippet in the head, or render it on the server from the cookie. Setting it in a client effect after hydration causes a flash of the wrong theme.
- Set `color-scheme` so native scrollbars and form controls match.

**Why custom properties**: they inherit and resolve at computed-value time, so switching a theme is one attribute change, with no re-render and no new stylesheet. Check contrast for every semantic pairing in both themes (WCAG 1.4.3: 4.5:1 for body text).

**Use a CSS transition when a property should animate between two states as a result of a change (hover, a class toggle); a keyframe animation when it should run by itself, loop or have intermediate steps (a spinner, a skeleton pulse, an entrance); and the Web Animations API when keyframes or timing are only known at runtime or you need control (pause, reverse, await completion), as in FLIP. All three use the same animation engine, so animating transform and opacity matters more than which API you pick.**

**Transitions**
```css
.menu { opacity: 0; translate: 0 -4px; transition: opacity 150ms ease-out, translate 150ms ease-out; }
.menu.open { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
```
Triggered by a computed value changing. List properties explicitly; `transition: all` animates unintended properties and can make theme switches lag. Entering from `display: none` needs `@starting-style` and `transition-behavior: allow-discrete` (newer CSS; treat as an enhancement where unsupported).

**Keyframes**
```css
@keyframes spin { to { rotate: 1turn; } }
.spinner { animation: spin 800ms linear infinite; }
```
Good for loops, multi-step sequences, staggered entrances (`animation-delay` per item) and scroll-driven effects (`animation-timeline: view()`, where supported).

**Web Animations API**
```js
const a = el.animate(
  [{ transform: `translateY(${dy}px)` }, { transform: "none" }],
  { duration: 220, easing: "ease-out" },
);
await a.finished;      // sequence with promises
a.pause(); a.reverse(); a.playbackRate = 2;
```
Same compositor benefits as CSS; you compute values in JavaScript (measured positions, drag velocity), and `document.getAnimations()` lets tests or tools inspect running animations.

**When a library is worth it**
Motion (formerly Framer Motion) and similar libraries add springs, gesture-driven animation, exit animations for unmounting React components and automatic layout (FLIP) animations. Worth the bytes for highly interactive apps; overkill for a marketing site where CSS covers it.

**Rules that apply to all three**
- Animate `transform` and `opacity`; avoid `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin`.
- Durations of about 100 to 200ms for feedback, 200 to 400ms for larger movement; ease-out for entering.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Do not block input on animation completion.

**FLIP stands for First, Last, Invert, Play: measure an element's position before a layout change, apply the change and measure again, apply a transform that visually puts it back where it started, then animate that transform to none. You use it to animate layout changes (list reorders, an item expanding into a detail view, grid filtering) smoothly, because layout runs once up front and only transform is animated.**

**Why it is needed**
Animating `top`, `left`, `width` or grid position forces layout and paint every frame, which janks on large pages or slow phones. Transform runs on the compositor. FLIP converts a layout change into a transform animation.

```js
function flipAll(container, mutate) {
  const items = [...container.children];
  const first = new Map(items.map((el) => [el, el.getBoundingClientRect()])); // read all
  mutate();                                                                   // one layout change
  for (const el of items) {
    const f = first.get(el);
    const l = el.getBoundingClientRect();                                     // read all again
    const dx = f.left - l.left, dy = f.top - l.top;
    if (!dx && !dy) continue;
    el.animate(
      [{ transform: `translate(${dx}px, ${dy}px)` }, { transform: "none" }],
      { duration: 250, easing: "ease-out" },
    );
  }
}
```

**Details that separate good answers**
- **Batch reads and writes.** Interleaving `getBoundingClientRect()` with style writes across many elements causes layout thrashing (forced synchronous layout per element). Read everything, mutate, read everything, then animate.
- **Scale distorts content.** If size changes, the inverted `scale()` stretches text and borders. Counter-scale the children, animate a wrapper, or cross-fade content.
- **Timing.** Measuring costs a frame or so; Paul Lewis, who named the technique, points out that users do not notice a short delay right after their input, so spend it on the setup.
- **Interruptions.** If a new change starts mid-animation, measure from the current visual position (cancel running animations first, or read the transformed rect).
- **Reduced motion.** Skip the animation and let items appear in place.

**Modern alternatives**
View transitions do FLIP-like work natively: give elements a `view-transition-name`, update the DOM inside `document.startViewTransition`, and the browser animates position and size. Motion's `layout` prop and similar library features implement FLIP for React. Hand-written FLIP is still useful when you need fine control or support beyond where view transitions work.

**Detect the user's OS setting with the prefers-reduced-motion media query and reduce motion rather than remove all feedback: replace slides, zooms and parallax with short fades or instant changes, stop auto-playing motion, and keep state changes visible. Apply it in CSS and in JavaScript-driven animations.**

**Why it matters**
People with vestibular disorders can get dizziness, nausea or migraines from large or parallax motion. WCAG 2.3.3 (AAA) covers animation from interactions; 2.2.2 (Level A) requires a way to pause, stop or hide content that moves automatically for more than five seconds; 2.3.1 forbids flashing more than three times per second.

**CSS: two patterns**
```css
/* 1. Opt-in motion: still by default, animate only when there is no preference */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .panel { transition: translate 300ms ease-out; }
  html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
}

/* 2. Reduced alternative: swap movement for opacity */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .toast { animation-name: fade-in; }      /* instead of slide-in */
  .hero-video { display: none; }           /* show the poster image instead */
}
```
A global safety net that shortens every animation is common, but on its own it can break JavaScript that waits for `animationend` and removes helpful feedback, so design the reduced version deliberately.

**JavaScript**
```js
const mq = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
let reduce = mq.matches;
mq.addEventListener("change", (e) => { reduce = e.matches; });

function open(el) {
  if (reduce) { el.hidden = false; return; }
  el.hidden = false;
  el.animate([{ opacity: 0, transform: "translateY(8px)" }, { opacity: 1, transform: "none" }], { duration: 200 });
}
```
Animation libraries usually expose a hook or a global setting for this; use it rather than checking in every component.

**What to reduce**
- Large translations, zooms, rotations, parallax, scroll-jacking, smooth scrolling.
- Auto-playing carousels and background video (also provide a pause button for everyone).
- Keep: colour and opacity changes, focus indicators, small progress indicators.

**Testing**: toggle "Reduce motion" in macOS, iOS, Windows or Android settings, or emulate it in Chrome DevTools (Rendering drawer) and in Playwright with `reducedMotion: "reduce"`.

**The browser checks each control against its constraint attributes (required, type, min, max, step, minlength, maxlength, pattern) and exposes the result in a ValidityState object. On submit it blocks invalid forms, focuses the first invalid field and shows its own message bubble. I replace the UI, not the engine: add novalidate, call checkValidity() on submit, and render accessible inline errors from the validity flags, while always revalidating on the server.**

**The pieces**
```text
input.validity: valueMissing, typeMismatch, patternMismatch, tooShort, tooLong,
                rangeUnderflow, rangeOverflow, stepMismatch, badInput, customError, valid
input.validationMessage    localised browser message
form.checkValidity()       returns boolean, fires 'invalid' events, no UI
form.reportValidity()      same, plus the browser's bubbles
input.setCustomValidity(msg)  non-empty = invalid with msg; "" = valid again
```

**Custom UI on top of native constraints**
```ts
form.noValidate = true;
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  let firstInvalid: HTMLInputElement | null = null;
  for (const el of form.querySelectorAll<HTMLInputElement>("input, select, textarea")) {
    const error = document.getElementById(`${el.id}-error`)!;
    if (el.validity.valid) { el.removeAttribute("aria-invalid"); error.textContent = ""; continue; }
    el.setAttribute("aria-invalid", "true");             // error is linked via aria-describedby
    error.textContent = el.validity.valueMissing ? "This field is required." : el.validationMessage;
    firstInvalid ??= el;
  }
  if (firstInvalid) { e.preventDefault(); firstInvalid.focus(); }
});
```

**When to replace the default UI**
- Branded design: bubbles cannot be styled and show one error at a time.
- Long forms: an error summary at the top linking to each field.
- Cross-field rules (password confirmation) and async checks (username taken): use `setCustomValidity` or your own state.
- Messages: `pattern` errors are generic ("match the requested format"); write specific text.

**Timing and styling**
Validate on submit, and on blur for touched fields; while typing, only clear errors. Style with `:user-invalid`, which matches after interaction, not `:invalid`, which marks empty required fields red on page load.

**Security**: client validation is UX only. The server must validate again (for example with a shared zod schema in a Server Action) and return field errors that the same UI can display.

**Keep the real native input in the DOM so focus, keyboard, form submission and the accessibility tree keep working, and change only its appearance: accent-color for a quick brand colour, or appearance: none plus your own drawing on the input itself or its pseudo-elements for full control. Then check focus-visible styles and forced-colors (Windows High Contrast) mode.**

**Level 1: accent-color**
```css
:root { accent-color: var(--color-primary); }
```
Recolours checkboxes, radios, range sliders and progress bars; the browser picks a contrasting checkmark colour.

**Level 2: appearance: none on the real input**
```css
input[type="radio"] {
  appearance: none;
  inline-size: 1.25rem; block-size: 1.25rem;
  border: 2px solid currentColor; border-radius: 50%;
  display: inline-grid; place-content: center;
}
input[type="radio"]::before {
  content: ""; inline-size: 0.6rem; block-size: 0.6rem; border-radius: 50%;
  background: CanvasText; scale: 0; transition: scale 120ms ease-out;
}
input[type="radio"]:checked::before { scale: 1; }
input[type="radio"]:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-ring); outline-offset: 2px; }
```
Checkboxes and radios with `appearance: none` accept `::before`, unlike text inputs. Stephanie Eckles's Modern CSS Solutions walks through this for checkboxes, radios and selects.

**Selects**
Style the closed control: `appearance: none`, padding, border, and a background-image arrow, keeping the native dropdown (which is excellent on mobile). Customisable select (`appearance: base-select`, which lets you style the picker and options) is arriving in Chromium; use it as progressive enhancement. A fully custom listbox is justified only when options need rich content, and then it must follow the APG combobox or listbox pattern with full keyboard support.

**Do not**
- Hide the input with `display: none` or `visibility: hidden` and draw a span: it leaves the tab order and the accessibility tree. If you must layer a visual on top, hide the input with a visually-hidden technique and keep it focusable.
- Remove outlines without a `:focus-visible` replacement.
- Rely on colour alone for checked or invalid states.

**Forced colors**
Windows High Contrast replaces your colours with system colours and drops backgrounds, so a checkmark drawn with `background` can vanish. Use system colour keywords (`CanvasText`, `Highlight`) or borders, and fix gaps with:
```css
@media (forced-colors: active) {
  input[type="checkbox"]:checked::before { background: Highlight; }
}
```

**Other modern helpers**: `field-sizing: content` for auto-growing textareas (Chromium for now), `color-scheme` so native controls match dark mode, and `::file-selector-button` for file inputs.
